# Copy to .env (never committed) and fill in your Fi app login. This is the
# same email and password you use in the Fi app on your phone. It drives
# both `kona probe` and the Activity tab in `kona serve`.
FI_EMAIL=
FI_PASSWORD=
# How often the app asks Fi for fresh numbers. Fi's API is undocumented and
# not ours; do not set this low.
#
# Three clocks decide how old the position on screen can be, and this is only
# the middle one:
#   1. the collar reports to Fi every few minutes on its own schedule
#      (`nextLocationUpdateExpectedBy` in a probe dump says when it next will)
#   2. this server asks Fi at most once per KONA_FI_REFRESH_SECONDS
#   3. an open Activity page collects the server's answer once a minute
# At the default 300 that is up to ~9 minutes behind the collar, which is why
# a walk can look like it never happened. 120 roughly halves the middle clock
# without asking Fi more often than the collar itself reports. Pull-to-refresh
# bypasses this setting, with its own 30-second floor.
KONA_FI_REFRESH_SECONDS=300
# The cadence while she is out: either "Start walk" is pressed on the page,
# or Fi itself reports a walk in progress. Fi's own GPS rate setting decides
# how fresh its answer can be -- on the 2026-09-13 walk the collar's last
# report was about a minute old -- so asking much faster than this buys
# requests, not freshness. Never goes below 10 seconds whatever is set here.
KONA_FI_LIVE_SECONDS=20
# How long one press of "Start walk" lasts before it switches itself off.
# A button nobody remembers must not poll Fi all night; two hours is longer
# than any walk and short enough that forgetting costs an afternoon.
KONA_FI_LIVE_MAX_SECONDS=7200
# Ignore aggregates from an older or unworn collar before this local date.
# Leave blank if every reading on the Fi pet profile belongs to this setup.
KONA_FI_DATA_START=

# Web app (kona serve). Passcode is shared by everyone who may watch Kona.
KONA_PASSCODE=
# Any long random string; keeps logins valid across restarts. Generate one with:
#   PowerShell:  -join ((48..57)+(97..122) | Get-Random -Count 40 | % {[char]$_})
KONA_SECRET=
# Only behind a tunnel or reverse proxy (docs/remote-access.md), never on a
# plain LAN. The header that carries the real visitor address -- cloudflared
# sets CF-Connecting-IP -- so the login lockout counts per visitor instead of
# treating everyone as 127.0.0.1. Unset = the peer address, forwarded headers
# ignored.
KONA_TRUSTED_PROXY_HEADER=
# The header is believed only from these peers: loopback, where cloudflared
# runs. Add the tunnel host's address if it ever runs on another machine.
# Never a LAN range -- a Wi-Fi visitor must not get to pick their own bucket.
KONA_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=127.0.0.1,::1
# Marks the session cookie Secure, so it only travels over HTTPS.
#   true  -- reaching the app through a Cloudflare tunnel (https://)
#   false -- LAN (http://192.168.x.x:8000) or Tailscale (http://100.x.y.z:8000)
# Get this wrong and there is no error to find: the browser refuses to send a
# Secure cookie over http, so a correct passcode just bounces back to the
# login screen. Flip it back to true before the next tunnel session, or the
# cookie travels a public HTTPS URL unprotected. docs/remote-access.md has
# both paths.
KONA_SECURE_COOKIES=false
# A folder for a rotating kona.log (access log, camera and Fi failures), so
# "it was broken this morning" has an answer after the console has closed.
# Blank = console only.
# Record what every Fi refresh saw, into a SQLite file at this path. Blank
# means record nothing, which is the default only because starting a web
# server should not begin writing files nobody asked for.
#
# Turn it on. Fi's hour-by-hour detail is today-only -- `pet_hourly()` takes no
# cursor -- so at midnight her time the day that just happened stops existing
# anywhere we can reach. Every day this stays blank is a day that cannot be
# recovered. About 15-20 MB a year. See docs/recording.md.
#
# The file is gitignored and must stay that way: a year of a dog's GPS history
# is a year of her owners' movements, and this repository is public.
KONA_DB_PATH=
KONA_LOG_DIR=
# Windows only. Holds off sleep while `kona serve` is running, instead of
# switching sleep off in the power plan for good. Stop the app and the PC
# sleeps normally again, so you only pay for the hours you are serving. The
# screen still sleeps either way, which is where most of the power goes.
KONA_KEEP_AWAKE=false
# Basemap for the location map. `osm` needs no account and is the default.
# `stadia` is Stadia Maps' Alidade Smooth Dark -- natively dark rather than a
# light map with a CSS filter over it, and retina-sharp on a phone. It needs a
# free key from client.stadiamaps.com (no card; 200k credits/month). Note the
# free tier is NON-COMMERCIAL use only.
#
# The key is gitignored like every other secret here, but it is NOT secret
# from a viewer: browser tiles carry it in the URL, so anyone who can load the
# page can read it. What it grants is this account's tile quota and nothing
# else. Domain-based auth avoids that and needs a stable hostname, which the
# Cloudflare quick tunnel does not give you -- see docs/remote-access.md 3a.
KONA_MAP_TILES=osm
KONA_STADIA_API_KEY=
# A ping URL from a dead-man's-switch service (healthchecks.io's free tier
# works). The app pings it every few minutes; when the pings STOP -- PC
# asleep, crashed, unplugged, offline -- the service emails you. That is the
# one failure nothing on this machine can report, because the machine is the
# thing that died. Treat the URL as a secret: whoever has it can fake the
# heartbeat and silence the alarm. Blank = no heartbeat.
KONA_HEARTBEAT_URL=
KONA_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=300
# usb (webcam on this machine) | rtsp (network camera, e.g. Tapo TC73) | fake
KONA_CAMERA_SOURCE=usb
# Which model, so the app shows only the controls it really has.
# c120/c110/c100 = fixed. c210/c220/c225 = pan and tilt. Blank = video only.
KONA_CAMERA_MODEL=
# usb: `uv run kona cameras` prints the indexes that open.
KONA_CAMERA_INDEX=0
# Picture size and capture rate. These are the only bandwidth levers that
# exist without a code change, and bandwidth is the thing that bites over the
# tunnel: at 1280x720 one viewer costs roughly 340 KB/s, about 1.2 GB an hour.
# Dropping to 640/480 trades detail for a smoother picture, though it also
# raises the frame rate, so measure bytes per second rather than frame size.
# docs/scaling-limits.md has the numbers. JPEG quality is fixed at 80 in code.
#
# usb: asks the webcam for this size. rtsp: the camera sends what it sends
# (the Tapo C120 main stream is 2560x1440, ~430 KB a frame) and the server
# shrinks each frame to KONA_CAMERA_WIDTH wide before sending it on, height
# following the aspect ratio, so /stream1's sharp picture arrives at a sane
# size. KONA_CAMERA_HEIGHT and FPS are ignored for rtsp. Set WIDTH=0 to pass
# the camera's full size through.
KONA_CAMERA_WIDTH=1280
KONA_CAMERA_HEIGHT=720
KONA_CAMERA_FPS=15
# rtsp: bare URL, no credentials in it. Tapo: rtsp://<camera-ip>:554/stream1 (HD)
# or /stream2 (SD). User/password = the *camera account* set in the Tapo app
# (Advanced Settings > Camera Account), not your TP-Link login.
KONA_RTSP_URL=
KONA_RTSP_USER=
KONA_RTSP_PASSWORD=
# The camera's switches (night vision, privacy mode, status light) use the
# Tapo local control API, which on recent firmware authenticates as user
# `admin` with your *TP-Link cloud* password -- the one you sign in to the
# Tapo app with, not the camera account above. Leave blank and the switches
# are simply not offered; nothing else changes.
KONA_TAPO_USER=admin
KONA_TAPO_PASSWORD=
# Recent firmware wants BOTH credentials: the camera account above for most
# calls, and your TP-Link *cloud* password -- the one you sign in to the Tapo
# app with -- to establish the session. Passing only one is what "Invalid
# authentication data" looks like. Leave this blank and the password above is
# used for both, which is what older firmware wants.
KONA_TAPO_CLOUD_PASSWORD=
# The robot (Hiwonder TurboPi) as a second camera, on its own Robot tab.
# Its stream server hands out one JPEG per GET at `?action=snapshot`; the
# app polls that through its own hub, so the phone never talks to the robot
# and the robot needs no credentials of its own. Leave the URL blank and
# there is no Robot tab and nothing else changes. Measured multi-reader on
# 2026-09-13, so watching it here never blocks anything else watching it.
KONA_ROBOT_SNAPSHOT_URL=
# What the tab and the header call it.
KONA_ROBOT_NAME=Robot
# Frames per second to ask the robot for. Each frame is one HTTP request to
# the Pi over Wi-Fi. The robot's own ceiling is about 19, and its camera runs
# in a different process from the drive gateway -- so this buys a smoother
# picture without taking anything from the controls. 15 by default; push it
# toward 19 if your Wi-Fi holds up, drop it if the robot's battery does not.
#
# It will not fix glass-to-glass latency on its own. That is the camera's own
# capture and encode time plus the network, and the honest way to measure it
# is to point the robot at a phone showing a stopwatch.
KONA_ROBOT_FPS=15
# Driving. Both of these, or neither: with them blank the Robot tab is a
# camera and there is no way to move anything, which is the right state
# until the Pi-side safety gateway is installed and proven.
#
# That gateway is the ONLY thing this app ever talks to on the robot -- never
# its raw JSON-RPC port 9030. It owns the watchdog that zeroes the motors
# when commands stop arriving, which has to live on the Pi because the
# failure it guards against is this PC or your phone becoming unreachable.
#
# Fill these in only after `gateway_watchdog_proof.sh` has passed on the real
# robot, on a stand. docs/first-run.md section 4c has the order.
KONA_ROBOT_CONTROL_URL=
# The shared secret the gateway's installer prints. It goes on every request
# as a header, over plain HTTP on your LAN: anyone who can sniff the Wi-Fi
# can read it and drive the robot. Fine for a home network with no port
# forwarding, but it is a trade, not an absence of risk.
KONA_ROBOT_TOKEN=
# Seconds without a new frame before the picture is shown as stale / the
# connection is considered hung and reopened.
KONA_STALE_SECONDS=3
KONA_HANG_SECONDS=10
# How long the camera stays open after the last viewer leaves, and the least
# time between releasing it and opening it again. A webcam closed and
# reopened within seconds is how a USB device gets wedged (opens fine, LED
# on, delivers nothing until replugged), so switching to Activity for a
# moment must not close it. The LED stays on for this long after you leave.
KONA_CAMERA_IDLE_SECONDS=120
KONA_CAMERA_REOPEN_SECONDS=2
